// @error expected (i32) => {q: f32} | null, got (i32) => {q: f64} | null
// THE ONLY CORPUS FILE THAT REACHES `nullableRetName`'s INLINE-SHAPE leg, and it is here to
// make that leg measurable rather than to pin a new verdict.
//
// B202 censused the compiler for sites that RENDER a type and then ask the rendering a
// question; `typecheck.vl`'s `if nameIsShapeOpen(inm)` was the last one standing, and B204
// had to record it as UNREACHED — 0 corpus files, and two witnesses built from the leg's own
// comment ("an INLINE shape with no matching TypeDecl") missed it. Neither was wrong about the
// shape; both were wrong about the ROUTE. The leg needs a CONTEXTUALLY-typed lambda whose
// inferred return is a nullable inline shape, which is what `each(...)` below is — a plain
// annotated return or an array-element spelling reaches `nominalNameOfObj` or nothing at all.
//
// The mismatch is deliberate and is the whole point. `{q: 1.5}` infers `{q: f64}` against the
// parameter's `{q: f32}`, and `nullableRetName` is what spells the "got" half of the message,
// so the leg runs and its rendering is visible in the expected text above. The same program
// with `f64` on both sides COMPILES AND RUNS — and never reaches the leg, which is why the
// working twin is not the fixture.
function each(f: (i32) => { q: f32 } | null) {
  const v = f(1)
  if v != null { print(v.q) }
}
each((k) => {
  if k == 0 { return null }
  return { q: 1.5 }
})
